 "text": "**Monzo is usually the simpler and more predictable choice for most UK trips to Europe. Revolut can be better if you travel often, spend heavily, or want to hold euros.**\n\nEurope (especially the eurozone) is very card-friendly, so fees on spending matter more than cash for most people. Both beat high-street banks, which often charge 2–3% plus ATM fees.\n\n### Card spending abroad\n| | **Monzo (free)** | **Revolut Standard (free)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fee on card payments | None | None within limits |\n| Rate | Mastercard rate (no markup) | Revolut rate (close to mid-market on weekdays) |\n| Weekend surcharge | None | 1% (roughly Friday evening–Sunday evening) |\n| Monthly FX limit | None | £1,000 fee-free on weekdays, then 1% |\n\nMonzo has no caps or weekend gotchas. Revolut’s rate can be excellent on weekdays within the allowance, but the weekend markup and £1,000 fair-usage limit catch people out on longer or higher-spend trips. You can reduce this by exchanging pounds to euros in the app on a weekday and spending from a euro balance.\n\n### ATM withdrawals in Europe (EEA)\nThe EEA covers EU countries plus Iceland, Liechtenstein and Norway.\n\n- **Monzo**: Unlimited fee-free if it is your main account (typically £500+ paid in plus an active Direct Debit in a rolling period, or certain benefits/student loan). Otherwise £400 fee-free every rolling 30 days, then 3%.\n- **Revolut Standard**: Free up to £200 or 5 withdrawals per month (whichever comes first), then 2% (minimum £1). Weekend markup can also apply to the conversion unless you pre-exchanged.\n\nLocal ATM operators can still charge their own fee on either card—use fee-free machines where possible.\n\n### Other practical differences\n- **Holding currency**: Revolut lets you hold euros (and many other currencies) and spend from that balance. Monzo converts everything to pounds.\n- **Protection**: Both are UK banks. Eligible deposits are FSCS-protected up to £120,000. Revolut completed its full UK banking licence in 2026 and is migrating customers in stages—check in the app whether your account is already with Revolut Bank UK Ltd.\n- **Everyday UK use**: Monzo is stronger as a main current account (pots, salary sorter, overdraft, budgeting). Revolut is stronger as a multi-currency travel tool.\n- **Paid plans**: Revolut Premium/Metal remove the FX limit and weekend fee and add higher ATM allowances plus extras (insurance, lounge access on higher tiers). Monzo’s paid plans mainly raise non-EEA cash limits and add perks/insurance. For pure Europe trips on free plans, the paid upgrade is less necessary with Monzo.\n\n### Which to pick\n- **Occasional trips, mostly card, some cash, want zero surprises** → **Monzo**. Especially strong if you can meet the “main account” criteria for unlimited free EEA cash withdrawals.\n- **Frequent travel, higher monthly spend, or you like holding euros and timing exchanges** → **Revolut** (use the free plan carefully, or a paid plan if the limits would cost you more than the subscription).\n- **Many people use both**: Monzo (or Starling) as the everyday UK account and a free Revolut as a travel wallet.\n\n**Always decline dynamic currency conversion** (the option to pay in pounds at the terminal or ATM). Choose euros/local currency for the better rate.\n\nFees and allowances change, so double-check the current figures in each app or on their fee pages before you travel. For a pure “no thinking required” Europe card, Monzo is the safer default for most UK residents.",
